<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.1.2: The Relationship between Length and Area</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01740</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.1.2: The Relationship between Length and Area</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9c9719a5-1b1c-4f14-9b18-583b7e6a5d89</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Noah has 50 meters of fencing to completely enclose a rectangular garden in the backyard.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="vegetable garden"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/681b4463fbcc0964ea81713e460b36c4fd9d81a6"/></media></figure></para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Draw some possible diagrams of Noah’s garden. Label the length and width of each rectangle.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Your answers will vary. Did you consider organizing your lengths, widths, and areas into a table? For example:</para>
  <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Length (meters)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Width (meters)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Area (square meters)</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>5</entry>
        <entry>20</entry>
        <entry>100</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>10</entry>
        <entry>15</entry>
        <entry>150</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>12</entry>
        <entry>13</entry>
        <entry>156</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>12.5</entry>
        <entry>12.5</entry>
        <entry>156.25</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>18</entry>
        <entry>7</entry>
        <entry>126</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>20</entry>
        <entry>5</entry>
        <entry>100</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>24</entry>
        <entry>1</entry>
        <entry>24</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</solution></exercise>
</example>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find the length and width of a rectangle that would produce the largest possible area. Explain why you think that pair of length and width gives the largest possible area.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>When the length and width are each 12.5 meters, the area is the largest (156.25 square meters). A table shows that as the rectangular shape gets closer and closer to being a square, the area gets larger.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
